#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199272} :activity/id #uuid "64d036ec-bd13-5db0-909a-44c4f2ba1ce6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257 :topic/id #uuid "8c7bb5de-2e24-552b-9fb3-2501f210a725" :topic/math-academy-id 4149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198240 :question/id #uuid "d154ea1a-bb3c-510d-abe3-dc1cf51558b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048225}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404169}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "2f924b6e-2f47-553e-b2e6-6d98b8c55641" :step/math-academy-id 26205 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199255 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199456 :tutorial/content "Consider a transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe points in the $(u,v)$ plane where $\\mathbf T$ is *locally* non-invertible are known as **critical points**.\n\nWe've already seen that $\\mathbf T$ is locally non-invertible when the Jacobian determinant is zero. Therefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf T$ are the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}=0.$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points of the following transformation:\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =4u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =|\\begin{aligned}4u & -6v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =4u\\cdot 1-(-6v)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ & =4u-6v.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe function has critical points when the Jacobian equals zero. So, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u-3v & =0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf{T}$ consist of those points that lie on the line $2u-3v=0$ in the $uv$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c544e3d3ecbdeaf7dc372b40ee0f37cb14e3ba292b8180a263c91103a9efe61.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f47e688-774b-5c89-a90d-ead52a871598" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4312 :tutorial/title "Critical Points of Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "16b2c818-03e6-5801-97b2-0ce8d225c3d5" :step/math-academy-id 26206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199466 :tutorial/content "Let's now state the so-called **inverse function theorem** for single-variable functions:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* $$\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the graph below:\n\n![](images/4d/4dfd8b142b594a2ca4f0e39f9b8ca1e04018bf0efd63f9dc2235bd61e9f9d70e.png)\n\nIn this example, the function $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ and has a nonzero derivative. As a result, $f^{-1}$ exists in a neighborhood of $x=b$ and is continuously differentiable at this point.\n\nOn the other hand, if $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ but $f'(a) = 0,$ then one of two things can happen:\n\n- **Case 1**: The function $f$ does not have a local inverse at $a.$\n\n![](images/04/04ee310e6068e517aec42a67fa83837986f8807e1250b89b52e7a62e3975bb38.png)\n\n In the example above, $f$ is not one-to-one in a neighborhood of $a,$ so it's not invertible there.\n- **Case 2**: The function $f$ has a local inverse at $a,$ but its inverse is not continuously differentiable at $b.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e2738a16f999d503fb295f4fd6535a854884468a4d190ffa537a4ec9295490c.png)\n\n In the example above, $f$ is invertible in a neighborhood of $a,$ but $f^{-1}$ is not differentiable at $x=b$ since it has a vertical tangent at this point." :tutorial/id #uuid "c45037f7-31c7-5911-a00d-edaa4c5b86e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7036 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "54cb06ef-1ffc-5b15-9147-a1baf70b985c" :step/math-academy-id 42404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198376 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198018 :question/id #uuid "f794d50a-cb44-5a6f-a922-8438a6192d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-11157"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351303}] :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "1b07afe2-9993-5d3c-bfe7-b98917224b3b" :step/math-academy-id 42423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199483 :tutorial/content "The inverse function theorem can be generalized to transformations too. We state it below:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf T:U \\to \\Bbb R^2$ be a $C^1$ (continuously differentiable) transformation on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ where* $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}], \\qquad \\mathbf{T}\\left([\\begin{aligned}{u}_{0} \\\\ {v}_{0}\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}].$$\n\n*Further, suppose that $(u_0,v_0)$ is **not** a critical point of $\\mathbf T.$* Then,\n\n- *$\\mathbf T$ is locally invertible at $(u_0,v_0),$*\n- *$\\mathbf T^{-1},$ the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ is a $C^1$ function in a neighborhood of $(x_0,y_0),$ and*\n- *the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} ,$ is given by* $$\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right)^{-1}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04daa2a7-b824-543f-b8d6-79a5b54e4799" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7678 :tutorial/title "The Inverse Function Theorem for Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "63b8e23b-b4a6-5984-b37f-dab9c1ced3af" :step/math-academy-id 44589 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198406 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198048 :question/id #uuid "217fe5bd-4ea4-57b9-8772-bb6e517207bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12416"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383567}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "809a242a-d30e-5e4d-9b3e-fc6725358aac" :step/math-academy-id 44598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199255}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Function Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198495}]}
